5.1. ReadArgs() explained 
 
   Release 2.0 introduced standard command line parsing. The ReadArgs() 
   routine is the heart of this feature. Each option should be a full 
   descriptive name (for example "QUICK" and not "Q"). Each option can be 
   prepended by an abbreviation of the form 'abbrev=option' ("Q=Quick"). 
   The argtemplate options are delimited by commas. Each option can also 
   be followed by modifiers that specify characteristics of individual 
   options. The valid modifiers are: 
 
       * /S - Switch 
 
         this is considered as a boolean variable, and will be set 
         if the option name appears in the command line. The entry is the 
         boolean (0 for not set, non-zero for set). 
 
 
       * /K - Keyword 
 
         this means that the option will not be filled unless the keyword 
         appears. For example if the template is "Name/K", then unless 
         "Name=<string>" or "Name <string>" appears in the command line, 
         Name will not be filled. 
 
 
       * /N - Number 
 
         this parameter is considered to be a decimal number, and will be  
         converted by ReadArgs(). If a number specified is invalid, ReadArgs() 
         will fail.  
 
 
       * /T - Toggle 
 
         similar to a switch (/S) but causes the boolean value to toggle. 
 
 
       * /A - Required 
 
         this keyword tells ReadArgs() to fail if this option is not specified 
         in the command line. 
 
 
       * /F - Rest of line 
 
         if this is specified, the entire rest of the line is taken as the 
         parameter for the option, even if other keywords appear in it. 
 
 
 
       * /M - Multiple 
 
         this means the option will take any number of arguments, returning 
         them as an array of pointers. Any arguments not considered to be part 
         of another option will be added to this option. Only one /M should 
         appear in a template. Example: for a template "Dir/M,All/S" the 
         command line "foo bar all qwe" will set the boolean "all", and return 
         an array consisting of "foo", "bar", and "qwe".  
 
   There is an interaction between /M parameters and /A parameters. If there 
   are unfilled /A after parsing ReadArgs() will grab strings from the end of a 
   previous /M parameter list to fill the /A's. This is used for things like 
   Copy("From/A/M,To/A"). 
 
 
6. FidoNet Technology 
 
6.1. Network structure 
 
    FidoNet is the largest amateur network in the world. It was started in 
   1984 by Tom Jennings. Currently more than 20000 nodes worldwide are 
   connected. The network has a hierarchic tree (or star) topology: 
 
                  +------------------------------+----------------------> 
                  |                              | 
                zone 1                         zone 2 
                  |                              | 
         +--------+--------+            +--------+--------+ 
         |        |        |            |        |        | 
       region   region   region       region   region   region 
         |        |        |            |        |        | 
      +--+--+     |     +--+--+     +---+---+    |     +--+--+ 
      |     |     |     |     |     |   |   |    |     |     | 
     net   net   net   net   net   net net net  net   net   net 
                        | 
                  +-----+-----+ 
                  |     |     | 
                node  node  node 
                 | 
             +---+---+ 
             |       | 
           point   point 
 
   Zones are divided in Regions, which are divided in Nets. The Nets 
   consist of Nodes, which are usually (but not necessarily) Bulletin 
   Board Systems. Each node has a unique address which consists of four 
   parts: Zone, Net, Node and Point, in text form expressed as 
   "zone:net/node.point". Zone numbers 1 up to and including 6 are used 
   by FidoNet: 
 
   1 = North America (United States of America and Canada) 
   2 = Europe and Commonwealth of Independent States 
   3 = Oceania (Australia and New Zealand) 
   4 = Latin America 
   5 = Africa 
   6 = Asia 
 
   There are several other networks that use FidoNet Technology and which 
   occupy higher zone numbers (such as NLA, zone 14). 
 
   Many nodes have one or more points. A point is a user who gets mail 
   from a node (it's "boss") in compressed files. That way they can read 
   and write messages offline, saving time and money. The point address 
   of the boss is 0, but the ".0" is usually omitted from the address. 
 
 
7. Request files explained 
  
    Rick Huebner, who adapted the ZModem routines for Opus, designed the 
   .REQ file-based file request system. 
 
 
7.1. REQ File 
 
   The .REQ file is simply a text file that contains the files we want 
   from the remote system. Those file names can include wildcards, but 
   should not contain a path. Optionally, there can be a password... if  
   the sending system requires one. The full template of a line in a REQ 
   file is: 
 
                  <filename><single-space-character>!<password><cr> 
 
   The password is limited to six characters for normal file request handlers, 
   however ZFServ does *not* have a limit on password size. 
 
   Let's say we're requesting ALLFILES.LHA and all node lists from 
   2:285/312. The file name would be 2.285.312.0.REQ.  The contents would 
   look like this: 
 
                  ALLFILES.LHA 
                  nodelist.* 
 
   If the sysop of 2:285/312.0 requires a password of THAT to get the file 
   ALLFILES.LHA, the REQ file contents would have to change: 
 
                  ALLFILES.LHA !that 
                  nodelist.* 
 
   If the request is of the "update" genre, the type of update and the 
   time, expressed as a UNIX-style long decimal ASCII number, follows the 
   name, or in the event that there is a transaction-level password, the 
   password. For example, an update request for file NEWFILES.*, where you 
   already have a file dated 1-January 1989, 00:00 and you live on the 
   East Coast (GMT+06) would be: 
 
                  NEWFILES.* +599634000 
 
   The sign is required, it indicates the type of update request. A '+' 
   means that all files matching the filespec "NEWFILES.*" newer than the 
   shown time will be sent, a '-' means that all matching files with dates 
   up to and including the indicated time will be sent. 
 
 
7.2. Mechanism 
 
   During the mail session, the .REQ file is simply transmitted to the 
   other system.  It goes "as is" like any other file. 
 
   The other system can ignore the request, send some of the files, or send 
   all of the files.  There is no accounting or responsibilities on the 
   part of the remote system. 
 
   If your implementation is unable to process the update information for any 
   reason, then you should process the line as a "regular" file request. 
 

 
8. Usage 
    
   The complete package uses a manager, a msg server, and two other utils. One
   allows you to view the overall statistics, and the other will list all the 
   systems that have requested, and their individual statistics. 
 
8.1. ZFSManager 
 
      ZFSManager is the filerequest handler, to get ZFServ running correctly you 
      must have ZFSManager running in the background. This is the heart of the 
      program, it will handle the requests ZFTell is sending to it's messageport. 
 
 
8.2. ZFTell 
 
      ZFTell is the program which sends the incoming request to the file 
      request handler, ZFSManager, and it also can set the various variables 
      needed for a flexible handler. ZFTell can take a few arguments in three 
      different modes which are 'COMMAND', 'SERVE' and 'QUIT'. These modes are 
      explained in the next paragraph. 
 
8.2.1. ZFTell COMMAND 
        
      ZFTELL COMMAND

      There are five keywords which can be used in the COMMAND mode, these  
      keywords are 'F=MAXFILES', 'B=MAXBYTES', 'U=UNLISTED', 'N=NOUNLISTED', 
      'D=DAILY' and S=STATUS. 
 
       F=MAXFILES/K defines the maximum of files which can be requested in one 
      request. 
 
      B=MAXBYTES/K defines the maximum of bytes which can be requested in one 
      file request. 
 
      U=UNLISTED/S lets systems request who are not listed in a nodelist at 
      your system. Note that if you want to let points from other systems, or systems
      not listed in the nodelists you have, file request from you, then you 
      must use this switch. 
 
      N=NOUNLISTED/S is the opposite of the UNLISTED switch. 
 
      D=DAILY will set the daily stats data file to zero. You could run this 
      from your cron each midnight. This has no effect on the Index file. A util
      for clearing and sorting the index file will be made avaiable.
 
      S=STATUS will return the settings of ZFSManager like the files, bytes  
      and (un)listed nodes. The status routines now call the CheckTime() function
      so the results you see are relevent only at the time you call ZFTELL command
      status.
 
      R=REINDEX will cause XFSManager to start to build or rebuild its custom
      fastindex. Output to the shell will list the dirs as they are scanned, and
      operations for sorting and alpha-indexing are carried out. You MUST use 
      this if you are using the USEQUICKINDEX keyword. During this operation,
      *NO* file requests can be handled. Disable inbound requests during this
      operation, or do only when the system is offline.
     
       *NOTE* If you are using TIMEGROUPS, the timegroup will always overide anything
       set using ZFTELL.

8.2.2. ZFTell SERVE

      ZFTELL SERVE

      This mode is the actual mode which handles incoming file requests.
      The template for this mode is:

      ZFTell SERVE I=IN/K/A,O=OUT/K/A,S=SPEED/K/N,A=ADDRESS/K/A,N=NAMEOFSYSOP/K/F/A

      IN              defines the incoming REQ file
      OUT             defines the outgoing RLO file
      SPEED        defines the connect baudrate
      ADDRESS      defines the address for the requesting system
      NAMEOFSYSOP  defines the name of the sysop of the requesting system.

      This is the line you add as the FREQ command to your Trapdoor.cfg or
      Node.config.

      Under TrapDoor, this translates to :

      FREQUEST "ZFtell serve \"i=%i o=%o s=%B a=%n n=%S\""

      For XMailer this translates into:

      FreqHandlerCommand ZFTell SERVE "i=%i o=%o s=%B a=%n n=%S"


8.2.3. ZFTell QUIT 
 
      ZFTELL QUIT

      The is the easiest mode of ZFTell, it just quits ZFSManager. 


8.3. ZFStat

      ZFSTAT

      This small Util will retrieve the currect statistics. It will list
      the totals of various items such as files sent etc.

8.4. ZFIndex
  
      ZFINDEX

      Allows you to display the Index file contents. All systems totals
      will be displayed, making it easy to see who has requested most etc.
 
 
9. Configuration file 
 
      Zeus:Config/ZFServ/ZFServ.CONFIG

      This file will set the definitions for ZFSManager. 
 
9.1. MYADDRESS 

      MYADDRESS 	
 
      This keyword defines the address which is used as the address of the 
      file request handler. 
 
9.2. SORRY 
 
      SORRY

      This is the name (and path) of the text file which is used when the 
      file request handler may not process incoming requests from unlisted 
      systems. 
 
9.3. REFUSED 
 
      REFUSED

      This is the name (and path) of the text file which is used when the 
      file request handler may not process incoming requests from this system. 
      This is controlled by the FORBID keyword. 
 
9.4. HEADER 
 
      HEADER

      This is the name (and path) of the text file which is used as header 
      for the message which is sent to the requesting system. 
 
9.5. FOOTER 
 
      FOOTER   

      This is the name (and path) of the text file which is used as footer 
      for the message which is sent to the requesting system. 
 
9.6. TEMPLATE

      TEMPLATE 
 
      This is the name (and path) of the text file which is used as text 
      for the message which is sent to the requesting system. Currently this 
      supports *no* Text Control Sequences. 
 
9.7. NEWMESSAGE

      NEWMESSAGE

      This is the name (and path) of the text file which is sent when 
      the requesting system requests for the first time. This file is sent as
      part of the message text.

9.8. MAGIC

      MAGIC

      This is the magic filename which defines the actual filename in your
      file catalogue, e.g. :

      MAGIC ALLFILES ALLFILES.LHA

      Multiple Magics are now supported. For example, if you wished to send more
      than one file with the same MAGIC, then just use the same MAGIC twice.
      e.g. :

      MAGIC ALLFILES ALLFILES.LHA
      MAGIC ALLFILES NEWFILES.LHA

      Would send both files to the request for ALLFILES.

      There is not other limit than memory set for this, use as much as you like.       
      

9.9. SPECMAGIC ( Specific Magic )

      SPECMAGIC

      This is the magic filename that defines the actual filename of ANY
      file on your system. e.g. :

      SPECMAGIC LHA c:lha

      Would send the file Lha from your C: directory to a request for LHA.

      Multiple SpecMagics are  supported. For example, if you wished to send more
      than one file with the same SPECMAGIC, then just use the same SPECMAGIC twice.
      e.g. :

      SPECMAGIC LHA c:lha
      SPECMAGIC LHA c:list

      Would send both C:Lha and C:List to a request for LHA. 


9.10. PASSWORD  
 
      PASSWORD

      This protects directories from illegal filerequests, i.e. only systems 
      that know the password can request files from that directory. 
      Example: 
 
      PASSWORD FILES:XENOL/ XenoMagic 
 
9.11. MAXBYTES 
 
      MAXBYTES

      This defines the maximum number of bytes that can be requested within 
      one file request. This can be altered while running ZFSManager by using the 
      ZFTell program. 
 
9.12. MAXFILES 
 
      MAXFILES

      This defines the maximum number of files that can be requested within 
      one file request. This can be altered while running ZFSManager by using the 
      ZFTell program. 

9.13. MAXTIME 
 
      MAXTIME

      This defines the maximum time of a file request session. Files may not be 
      requested that exceed this limit. Accounting is cumalative. If a callers 
      request lists 10 files, and the first exceeds this limit, then it will not be 
      sent. Each file is dealt with by comparing its download time with the time
      left after other files have been added to the RLO file. This can be 
      altered while running ZFSManager by using the  ZFTell program. 
 
9.14. NOUNLISTED 
 
      NOUNLISTED

      This forbids file requests from nodes (points) that are not in your 
      nodelist(s). Default (leaving out this keyword)  will allow any system 
      (apart from those defined with FORBID) to request files. 
 
9.15. MAXWILD 
 
      MAXWILD

      This defines the maximum number of files that match a wildcarded 
      filename. 
 
9.16. DEFAULTSEND 
 
      DEFAULTSEND

      This file will always be send to the system requesting a file, be careful 
      with this, as not everyone will like this :-). Example: 
 
      DEFAULTSEND FILES:12/NEWFILES.LHA 
 
9.17. FORBID 
 
      FORBID

      This forbids systems to file request, you can use wildcards as well, but 
      be careful with it, you can wipe out a whole net or even a zone. Example: 
 
      FORBID 2:2500/* 
 
9.18. PREVENT
        
      PREVENT

      This prevents files from this directory being requested. Use to protect
      sysop areas, beta areas or any area you do not wish to allow requests from.
      This only applies to Filecat areas, _NOT_ external indexes. Do not use a
      PASSWORD and PREVENT on the same area, PREVENT will win every time !

      PREVENT FILES:PRIVATE/

9.19. LOGFILE 
 
      LOGFILE

      This defines the name of the logfile to be used. 
 
 
9.20. DIRECTORY 
 
      DIRECTORY

      Support for the INDEX files on many CDROM's, it uses another external 
      utility which is called Scan, that has to be somewhere in your path. The 
      template of this line is: 
 
      DIRECTORY <device|assign> <indeZFile> 
 
      e.g. 
 
      DIRECTORY AMINET_0294 XCONFIG:CDROM/AMINET_0294/Index 
 
      The file Indexe above is created from the indeZFile of the AMinet CDROM, 
      the same method can be used for any device though. The format of the index 
      file *must* be: 
 
      Filename Path Size Description 
 
      Path should be the path without the device name, e.g. 'BIZ/DBASE', there 
      must be a space between each field. 
 
      The search is performed by the ultrafast Scan utility which is provided. 
      This utility scans a directory many times as fast as the SEARCH command  
      of AmigaDOS. The utility SCAN should be placed somewhere in your global path
      C: is suggested.
 
      Note: With this keyword you can also put files for request that are *not* 
            in your filebase, only create an indeZFile and put an assign to the 
            directory and that's it. 
 
 
9.21. ZFServPATH
       
      ZFServPATH

        This is new under 2.5, it defines the directory in which the INDEX and STATS
      files will be kept. Please note, you must have this, and the directory must 
      exist. e.g. :

      ZFSERVPATH ZEUS:CONFIG/ZFServ/
              

9.22. PKTPATH

      PKTPATH

      This defines the directory in which outbound msg packets will be created.
      This entry must be in your ZFServ.config, and it must exist. e.g. :
      
       PKTPATH T:
 
       See also KEEPPACKETS.

9.23. KEEPPACKETS

      KEEPPACKETS

       This is a switch. The default is NOKEEPPACKETS. This keyword in your 
       config file will cause XMAILER and TRAPDOOR not to delete outbound msg
       packets generated by ZFSManager.  This is for those of you who wish to 
       keep outbound packets. All kept packets will be filenoted with the address
       and the SysopName of the requesting system. Thus allowing you to see who 
       requested what.
      
9.24. NOINDEX

      NOINDEX

      This is a switch. The default is INDEX. This keyword in your config
      will switch off the INDEX operations. The Index is used for keeping a
      record of the systems that have requested, and thier daily accounts.
      The index file is currently set to hold 3000 systems. This is systems,
      not requests. A small tool will be avaiable to list all the data within
      the Index file in the near future.

9.25. DMAXFILES
  
      DMAXFILES

      Providing you have have an INDEX, this keyword defines the maximum 
      number of files a system may request in any one day. Thus preventing
      systems from using multiple requests to get around your session limits.


9.26. DMAXBYTES

      DMAXBYTES

      Providing you have have an INDEX, this keyword defines the maximum 
      number of bytes a system may request in any one day. Thus preventing
      systems from using multiple requests to get around your session limits.

9.27. NOFASTINDEX

      NOFASTINDEX

      Usage of the Index routines means that data must be scanned during, and
      the Index file updated, after every request. There are two methods
      employed in ZFSManager to do this. FASTINDEX loads the whole Index file
      into memory when ZFSManager is started, thus speeding up the index
      search when an incoming request occurs. NOFASTINDEX reads the data from
      the HD when required. Both only update the Index file AFTER the request
      has been served, thus not holding back the file(s) being sent. The Index
      file is some 70K in size, and this is the amount of memory taken if an
      Index is used. The default is FASTINDEX, thus using the Index in memory
      method.

        
9.28. TRAPLIST

      TRAPLIST
  
      Keyword disabled. Traplist support is always switched on. 
     
      
9.29. FREEAREA

      FREEAREA

      This will make all files from the given directory free. Bytes and files
      will not be counted. Useful for filelists, and support areas etc.
      MAXTIME & DMAXTIME settings are not affected.

      FREEAREA FILES:XENOL/

9.30. STRING*

      STRING*

        From V2.60 Onwards, most strings can be defined. These strings are used
      where a file cannot be found, or some reason exists why the file cannot
      be sent. Usage is as follows. e.g.

      STRING* This is the string to use.
      
      
      You may alter the strings. You may NOT alter the keywords. The maximum
      length for any string is 40 characters. Anything exceeding this will
      be truncated.

      The Kewwords and strings are as follows.

9.30.1. STRINGMAXBYTES 
  
      STRINGMAXBYTES     

      Defines the string used when the number of bytes exceeds your maxbytes
      session limit.

      Suggested string = 'Maximum Bytes for this session exceeded.'
      
      
9.30.2. STRINGMAXFILES 

      STRINGMAXFILES 

      Defines the string used when the number of files exceeds your maxfiles
      session limit.
 
      Suggested string = 'Maximum Files for this session exceeded.'


9.30.3. STRINDGMAXFILES
  
      STRINDGMAXFILES

      Defines the string used when the number of files exceeds your daily
      dmaxFiles limit.
 
      Suggested string = 'Maximum Files for today exceeded.


9.30.4. STRINDGMAXBYTES 

      STRINDGMAXBYTES 

      Defines the string used when the number of files exceeds your daily
      dmaxbytes limit.
 
      Suggested string = 'Maximum Bytes for today exceeded.


9.30.5. STRINGTIMEEXCEEDED 

      STRINGTIMEEXCEEDED 

       Defines the string used when the session time exceeds your maxtime
      session limit.
 
      Suggested string = 'Maximum session time exceeded.


9.30.6. STRINGINVALID 

      STRINGINVALID 

      Defines the string used when the file requested could not be found,
      or no match could be found if a wildcard was used.
 
      Suggested string = 'This file not found or password error.


9.30.7. STRINGUPDATE 

      STRINGUPDATE 

      Defines the string used when an update request has been received, and 
      no file that complies to the update request has been found.
 
      Suggested string = 'Update Request - Nothing found.


9.30.8. STRINGUNKNOWN 

      STRINGUNKNOWN 

      Normal files usually have a prefix, seperator, and suffix, 
      e.g. 'Blah.Lha'. The exception would be MAGICS, such as 'Files'.

      ZFSMANAGER looks at the requested filename, and should the file not 
      contain the 3 parts described above, and magic is found that matches
      replies that the filename is either an unknown MAGIC, or an illegal
      filename.
 
      Suggested string = 'Unknown MAGIC or illegal filename.

9.30.9. STRINGNODEVICE 

      STRINGNODEVICE 

      Defines the string used when the requested file was found, but the 
      device was offline, usually a CD-ROM or other removeable media device.
 
      Suggested string = 'File found. But device offline.

 
9.30.10. STRINGTOTALS 

      STRINGTOTALS 

      This string is used as a header to the totals strings, which give the 
      the totals of bytes and files a system has requested. This only applies if
      an Index is being used.

      e.g. Your system has requested the following :

9.30.11. STRINGNUMBYTES

      STRINGNUMBYTES

      The string used to give the total number of bytes a system has requested
      for ALL requests. Note the use of '%d'. This will be replaced by the numeric
      value.

9.30.12. STRINGNUMFILES

      STRINGNUMFILES

      The string used to giv the total number of files a system has requested
      for ALL requests. Again the '%d' is replaced by the numeric value.

      e.g. Total number of bytes : %d        


9.30.13. STRINGNODESCRIPTION

	When using the custom Quick Index routines with a CD, CD's do not have file-comments.
     This string will be used instead. Or when a file-comment is less then 6 characters long.


9.31.  USEFLO

     Switch - Default NOUSEFLO. With this set, all output is writtine to an *.HLO file rather
    then the default *.RLO file created by Trapdoor or Xmailer. The reason for this is that if
    a system drops carrier for any reason during the session, using the default *.RLO method would
    result in the request reply being lost, meaning the system would need to request them again.
    
     Using the *.HLO file will mean that the calling system would not need to request the files 
    again, as the *.HLO file is not deleted until all files have been succesfully sent. If a system
    should request the files again, XFSManager checks the *.HLO file for any matches before adding any
    new files to the *.HLO. The other advantage is that nothing is added to the systems account if the 
    file already exists in the *.HLO file.

     NOTE:
   
     You MUST have the following statement in Zfserv.config.

9.31.1  OUTBOUND

    Define your outbound path. This is used when the USEFLO switch is on. See USEFLO

   e.g. OUTBOUND XOUT:

9.32. GROUP

      GROUP

      Groups are now supported by ZFServ. This enables you to specify
      different limits for different nodes/groups of nodes. To use the group
      function, you must firstly give the GROUP a name e.g. 

      GROUP MYPOINTS

      You may the define the limits for this group using the keywords
 
      GMAXFILES  - same as MAXFILES but for this Group only.
      GMAXBYTES  -  "      "  MAXBYTES  "       "      "       "        "
      GMAXTIME      -  "      "  MAXTIME      "       "      "       "        "
      GDMAXFILES -  "      "  DMAXFILES "       "      "       "        "
      GDMAXBYTES -  "      "  DMAXBYTES "       "      "       "        "

      You may now define the nodes these limits apply to using the keyword

      GNODE 2:2500/167.*

      the above would translate to meaning all my points, as wild cards are
      accepted.

      The Group MUST end with the keyword

      GROUPEND

      Here's an example of a pair of complete group definitions.

      GROUP MYPOINTS
      GMAXBYTES 5000000
      GMAXFILES 10
      GMAXTIME  40
      GDMAXTIME 60
      GDMAXFILES 20
      GDMAXBYTES 10000000
      GNODE 2:2500/167.* 
      GROUPEND
  
      GROUP GOODNODES 
      GMAXBYTES 15000000
      GMAXFILES 100
      GMAXTIME  120
      GDMAXTIME 120
      GDMAXFILES 100
      GDMAXBYTES 150000000
      GNODE 2:250/151.0
      GNODE 2:441/93.0
      GROUPEND


9.33. TIMEGROUP

      TIMEGROUP

      TimeGroups are used to define variables and flags at during certain
      periods. Although you may still use an external Cron facility to change
      the internal variables if you wish, TIMEGROUPS will overide everything
      except GROUP statements. In other words, the TIMEGROUP statments become
      the defaults applied to any node not listed in a GROUP.

      A TimeGroup starts with the keyword TIMEGROUP followed by 3 arguments.
      ReadArgs() is used to parse these args, an error on your TIMEGROUP line
      will be flagged when the config is read.

      e.g

      TIMEGROUP D=DAY/A,H=HOUR/A,M=MIN/A

      DAY  or D = Numeric Value 0 = Sunday, 2 = Monday ...... 6 = Saturday ; 
      7 = EVERDAY

      HOUR or H = Numeric Value 0 - 23 ; 24 = Every Hour
      MIN  or M = Numeric Value 0 - 59 ; 60 = Every Minute
 
      TIMEGROUP D=0 H=18 M=0 ( Sunday 6:00 pm )
 
      Any of the MAX or DMAX variables can be used, preceded with 
      a T to denote TIMEGROUP
 
      eg TMAXTIME      = 30 sets Session Maximum time to 30 mins
      TDMAXTIME  = 60 Sets Daily Maximum time to 60 mins
 
      There is no limit to Number of Definitions. Each Group must end with ENDTIME

      Remember Groups are NOT affected by the timegroup statements.Only the 
      default settings.
        
        Here are some examples of TIMEGROUPS, and comments. This is taken from
      the example config file.

      
      * Anytime Group *
      TIMEGROUP D=7 H=24 M=60
      TMAXTIME  30
      TMAXFILES  20
      TMAXBYTES  4000000
      ENDTIME
      *
      * Alter at 6 pm( Anyday ), no unlisted nodes, and change certain settings.
      *
      TIMEGROUP D=7 H=18 M=0
      TMAXTIME  30
      TDMAXTIME  60
      TMAXFILES  10
      TMAXBYTES  2000000
      TNOUNLISTED
      ENDTIME
      *
      * On Sunday, at 9:00 am. Set TNOUNLISTED ( unlisted nodes prevented ), alter 
      * certain settings.
      *
      TIMEGROUP D=0 H=9 M=0
      TMAXTIME  30
      TDMAXTIME  60
      TMAXFILES  10
      TMAXBYTES  2000000
      TNOUNLISTED
      ENDTIME
      *
      * The same goes for Saturday as well.
      *
      TIMEGROUP D=6 H=9 M=0
      TMAXTIME  30
      TDMAXTIME  60
      TMAXFILES  10
      TMAXBYTES  2000000
      TNOUNLISTED
      ENDTIME
      *
      * On Sunday, at 5.00pm, allow unlisted again, and change settings.
      * 
      TIMEGROUP D=0 H=17 M=0
      TMAXTIME  30
      TDMAXTIME  60
      TMAXFILES  20
      TMAXBYTES  3000000
      ENDTIME
      *
      * And also on Saturday.
      *
      TIMEGROUP D=6 H=17 M=0
      TMAXTIME  30
      TDMAXTIME 60
      TMAXFILES 20
      TMAXBYTES 3000000
      ENDTIME

9.34. COMMAND

      COMMAND

      The COMMAND keyword begins a string which defines a password, and a command to 
      be executed. The line is parsed by readargs(), and the arguments are as follows

      M=Password C=Command

      e.g. COMMAND M=RUNMAIL C=run execute xscripts:incoming.1

      On receipt of the request containing the word RUNMAIL, the command
      'run execute xscripts:incoming.1' will be executed.

      Note the usage of this can be synchronous or async depending on the
      way you set the command string.

9.34.1  USEQUICKINDEX

       Keyword - USEQUICKINDEX - Default is NOUSEQUICKINDEX. Should you alter
     ANY of the QDIR's ( see below ), you must then use ZFTELL COMMAND REINDEX. 
     Failure to do this will result in the entries not being found. Whilst 
     re-indexing, XFServe will be unable to handle Freq's, it is suggested that
     Freq's are disabled whilst re-indexing is performed. The Custom Quick 
     Index routine is very fast, wild card searches are also allowed.

     See ZFTELL COMMAND REINDEX

9.34.2  QDIR

       KeyWord - QDIR - followed by the directories to add to the Index. Do
       not use / on the end of the entries.

	e.g. QDIR Aminet_0794:Aminet/biz/dbase

	You may add as many sub-dirs as you like. *NOTE* These are held in 
	a list in memory to increase speed.



9.35. EXAMPLE

      EXAMPLE Configuration

;  This config file MUST be in XCONFIG: Sorry, but this is required.
;
* Note you put your own primary address.
MYADDRESS 2:2500/167.0
*
*
* Path to use for index and stat files. MUST have '/' or ':' on the end !
*
ZFServPATH XCONFIG:ZFServ/
*
*
* PKTPATH - This is where reply packets will be put, normally T:, but if you wish to keep them,
*           make it somewhere on your HD. Again, end with '/' or ':'
*
PKTPATH T:
*
*If you wish to keep the pkts, then use KEEPPACKETS, default is no NOKEEPPACKETS
*
KEEPPACKETS
*
* The next five all relate to text files, examples provided,
* edit them to suit yourself.
*
*
SORRY  XCONFIG:ZFServ/ZFServ.sorry
REFUSED XCONFIG:ZFServ/ZFServ.refused
HEADER XCONFIG:ZFServ/FreqHeader
FOOTER XCONFIG:ZFServ/Footer
TEMPLATE XCONFIG:ZFServ/ZFServ.template
NEWMESSAGE XCONFIG:ZFServ/newsystem
*
*
* When using magics, only put the name of the file, NOT the full path.
*
* Wildcards may also be used in Magics. Note Multiple files can be sent by
* using the same MAGIC word. In the examples, the MAGIC 'files', sends both
* Files.lha & newfiles.lha
*
* N.B. A strange thing can happen with MAGIC wilcards. If you use nodediff.*, and you
* delete the old nodediff, i.e update it each week. Then until the filecat is rebuilt,
* it will look for the old nodediff too. To remedy this,its suggested that when ever
* a file is removed from the filecat, that section be rebuilt ASAP. I'll look into a 
* way of preventing this, but I don't wish to slow it down too much.
*
MAGIC files Files.Lha
MAGIC FILESG FILEGUIDE.LHA
*MAGIC files newfiles.lha
MAGIC FGUIDE FILEGUIDE.LHA
MAGIC newfiles NEWFILES.LHA
MAGIC NODELIST NodeList.Z*
MAGIC NODEDIFF Nodediff.*
MAGIC TRAPDOOR TD_1_84.LHA
MAGIC region Region25.*
MAGIC ACD2G  NET0294GUIDE.LHA
MAGIC ACD2   AMINET_0294.LHA
MAGIC Xenofiles XenoFiles.lha
MAGIC ZFServ  ZFServ*
*
*
* Password protect directories
*
PASSWORD FILES:XENOL/ Guesswhat
PASSWORD FILES:XENOL190/ Guesswhat
PASSWORD FILES:XENOG/ Guesswhat
PASSWORD FILES:XENOBETA/ GuessWhat
PASSWORD FILES:XDISTRIB/ GuessWhat
*
* Maximum bytes per request
* 
MAXBYTES 3000000
*
* Maximum files per request
*
MAXFILES 10
*
* Don't allow unlisted systems.
*
*NOUNLISTED
*
* Maximum number of wild card matches allowed.
*
MAXWILD 5
*
* Maximum Time limit
*
MAXTIME 30
*
* Send this file always
*
*DEFAULTSEND FILES:12/NEWFILES.LHA
*
* Forbid system from requesting
*
*  Wild cards allowed, be careful, you can wipe out a whole net, or a Zone wildcard.. everyone :)
*
FORBID 2:2500/167.23
*
* Prevent any requests from these directories ( only applies to Filecat areas )
*
PREVENT FILES:27/
PREVENT FILES:PRIVATE/
PREVENT FILES:XDISTRIB/
*
* This is obvious ..
*
LOGFILE XLOG:ZFServ.log
*
* Directory - Allows usage of external Index files, such as the Aminet Indexes.
* These may require editing ( Aminet's do ), each entry line in the index _MUST_
* use the following format
* 
* File                Dir        Size Description
*
* eg
*
* AddressMastr1_19.lha biz/dbase  301K comfortable Addressbook, OS 2.0+
*
* As you can see the first part of the DIRECTORY entry is the parent path,
* and then the path:/name of the index file. This was specifically added to
* allow requests from Aminet CD-ROMS. It can be used for almost anything though,
* as long as you understand the principle behind its operation.
*
DIRECTORY AMINET_0294:Aminet/ Xconfig:CDROM/Aminet_0294/Index
DIRECTORY AMINET_0794:Aminet/ Xconfig:CDROM/Aminet_0794/Index
*
* Maximum files a systemcan request per day 
*
DMAXFILES 30
*
* Maximum Bytes a system can request per day
*
DMAXBYTES 5000000
*
* Use Index, if no index, DMAXBYTES, and DMAXFILES not operative;
* Default is index on. Use NOINDEX to switch off.
* 
*NOINDEX
*
* Index type. Loads index into memory and reads from there,
*             NOFASTINDEX reads from disk. default is
*             FASTINDEX
FASTINDEX
*
*
* Start Group ; Set different levels for specific systems.
*
* GROUP  must be followed by GROUPNAME
* 
* Each entry in Group is the same as other entries, EXCEPT it is preceded by G
* Hence GMAXBYTES etc..
* The Group definition MUST end with ENDGROUP
*
GROUP MYPOINTS
GMAXBYTES 5000000
GMAXFILES 20
GMAXTIME  40
GDMAXTIME 60
GDMAXFILES 20
GDMAXBYTES 10000000
GNODE 2:2500/167.*
GROUPEND
*
GROUP GOODNODES
GMAXBYTES 15000000
GMAXFILES 100
GMAXTIME  120
GDMAXTIME 120
GDMAXFILES 100
GDMAXBYTES 150000000
GNODE 2:250/151.0
GNODE 2:441/93.0
GNODE 2:2500/168.1
GROUPEND
*
*
* Add Traplist support ( For Jorn ! ). Traplist support functions will only be called
* if Xeno's nodelist routines fail to locate a system. This adds support for certain
* external pointlists that are floating around.
*
*
*
* SPECMAGIC - Keyword defines a SPECIFIC path:/file to be sent. The filecat will NOT be searched
*             for this file. A check will be made to see if the path:/file exists though, before
*             anything is added to the .RLO and message.
*
*SPECMAGIC LHA c:lha
*SPECMAGIC LHA c:list
*
* FREEAREA - Defines if a certain Directory will be free, bytes and files not counted.
*            Time definitions STILL remain however.
* Usage :    FREEAREA Files:FileLists/
*
FREEAREA Files:12/
FREEAREA FILES:Xenol/
FREEAREA FILES:XENOG/
*
* Time Variables : ReadArgs() type primary line Starts Time definitions Group.
*
*  DAY = Numeric Value 0 = Sunday, 2 = Monday ...... 6 = Saturday ; 7 = EVERDAY
* HOUR = Numeric Value 0 - 23 ; 24 = Every Hour
* MIN  = Numeric Value 0 - 59 ; 60 = Every Minute
*
* No limit to Number of Definitions. Each Group must end with ENDTIME
*
* eg TIMEGROUP D=0 H=18 M=0 ( Sunday 6:00 pm )
*
* Any of the MAX or DMAX variables can be used, preceded with a T to denote TIMEGROUP
*
* eg TMAXTIME   = 30 sets Session Maximum time to 30 mins
*    TDMAXTIME  = 60 Sets Daily Maximum time to 60 mins
*
*    Groups are NOT affected by the timegroup statements.Only the default settings.
*    
*    Keep them in chronological order, if only to make the config file look neater !
*    The only exceptions are Anyday/Anytime groups, which must come first.
*
*  The first group here defines an anytime , all 7 days, 24 hours, 60 mins. Put this first, 
*  and it becomes the default settings. Any other group following that matches, will overide 
*  the first group. Put it last, and it will overide all the other timegroups !
*  
*  Any variable not set, e.g. in  the first group, no daily maximums's are set, thus the DMAX*
*  settings defined above will be in operation. NOUNLISTED, in a TIMEGROUP becomes TNOUNLISTED
*  If the keyword TNOUNLISTED is not in a TIMEGROUP, then UNLISTED nodes may request.
*
*  Remember, if a TIMEGROUP lower down the list matches, it will take over. Hence, the anytime
*  Group, is only valid if no other TIMEGROUP takes over. These Groups are checked at each 
*  request. 
*
* Anytime Group *
TIMEGROUP D=7 H=24 M=60
TMAXTIME  30
TMAXFILES  20
TMAXBYTES  4000000
ENDTIME
*
* Alter at 6 pm( Anyday ), no unlisted nodes, and change certain settings.
*
TIMEGROUP D=7 H=18 M=0
TMAXTIME  30
TDMAXTIME  60
TMAXFILES  10
TMAXBYTES  2500000
TNOUNLISTED
ENDTIME
*
* On Sunday, at 9:00 am. Set TNOUNLISTED ( unlisted nodes prevented ), alter 
* certain settings.
*
TIMEGROUP D=0 H=9 M=0
TMAXTIME  30
TDMAXTIME  60
TMAXFILES  10
TMAXBYTES  2000000
TNOUNLISTED
ENDTIME
*
* The same goes for Saturday as well.
*
TIMEGROUP D=6 H=9 M=0
TMAXTIME  30
TDMAXTIME  60
TMAXFILES  10
TMAXBYTES  2000000
TNOUNLISTED
ENDTIME
*
* On Sunday, at 5.00pm, allow unlisted again, and change settings.
* 
TIMEGROUP D=0 H=17 M=0
TMAXTIME  30
TDMAXTIME  60
TMAXFILES  20
TMAXBYTES  3000000
ENDTIME
*
* And also on Saturday.
*
TIMEGROUP D=6 H=17 M=0
TMAXTIME  30
TDMAXTIME 60
TMAXFILES 20
TMAXBYTES 3000000
ENDTIME
*
*
* String Defines. All strings except one are now definable. The maximum allowed length
* is 40 characters,except STRINGTOTALS which is 250 ( use normal C formatting eg %d\n). 
* Anything over this will be truncated.
*
STRINGMAXBYTES Maximum Bytes for this session exceeded.
*
STRINGMAXFILES Maximum Files for this session exceeded.
*
STRINDGMAXFILES Maximum Files for today exceeded.
*
STRINDGMAXBYTES Maximum Bytes for today exceeded.
*
STRINGTIMEEXCEEDED Maximum session time exceeded.
*
STRINGINVALID This file not found or password error.
*
STRINGUPDATE Update Request - Nothing found
*
STRINGUNKNOWN Unknown MAGIC or illegal filename
*
STRINGNODEVICE File found. But device offline.
*
STRINGTOTALS           Your system has requested the following :
*
STRINGNUMBYTES         Total number of bytes : %d
*
STRINGNUMFILES         Total number of files : %d
*
* Ok, Here's the new bit. This is the COMMAND function. The line is parsed by readargs(),
* So make sure you have it right ! M=MAGICWORD C=COMMAND TO EXECUTE. Obviously, you can use
* Run <Nil: >NIL: for async detatchable tasks, and a straight command for sync tasks.
* As usual, let me know if it does what you want or not.
*
COMMAND M=TESTIT C=run execute xscripts:incoming.1
COMMAND M=CALL128.1004 C=s:call 2:238/1002
*
* Use Flofile
*
USEFLO
*
* Define Outbound directory
*
OUTBOUND XOUT:
*
*
* End of Config
      
